Hierarchical Contextualized Representation for Named Entity Recognition
=======================================================================

This repository is a study of, and a set of maintenance fixes to, an existing
research codebase. It is not an independent reimplementation. The model code was
not written here; what was added is the test suite, the documentation, and the
corrections listed in README.md under "What changed in this repository".

Provenance
----------

1. Model, training loop and utilities

   The architecture implements:

       Ying Luo, Fengshun Xiao, Hai Zhao.
       "Hierarchical Contextualized Representation for Named Entity Recognition."
       Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 2020.

   The surrounding framework is derived from NCRF++, an open-source neural
   sequence labeling toolkit released under the Apache License 2.0:

       Jie Yang and Yue Zhang.
       "NCRF++: An Open-source Neural Sequence Labeling Toolkit." ACL 2018.
       https://github.com/jiesutd/NCRFpp

   The derivation is direct: main.py, utils/data.py, utils/alphabet.py,
   utils/functions.py, utils/metric.py, utils/tagSchemeConverter.py,
   model/seqlabel.py, model/wordsequence.py, model/wordrep.py and model/crf.py
   all originate there, and main.py's own argument parser still describes itself
   as "Tuning with NCRF++".

   model/IntNet.py, model/SentenceRep.py and model/MemoryBank.py implement the
   three components the AAAI 2020 paper contributes on top of that framework.

2. BERT/

   BERT/modeling.py, BERT/tokenization.py, BERT/extract_features.py and
   BERT/__init__.py are Google's original release, unmodified apart from added
   explanatory docstrings:

       Copyright 2018 The Google AI Language Team Authors
       https://github.com/google-research/bert
       Licensed under the Apache License, Version 2.0

   BERT/LICENSE is Google's licence file and applies to those four files.

   BERT/get_aligned_bert_emb.py and BERT/run.sh are not part of Google's release;
   they align BERT's word-piece output to token boundaries for this model.

3. sample_data/

   The four sentences in sample_data/eng.bioes.train are drawn from CoNLL-2003,
   which is distributed under its own terms and is not redistributed here. See
   sample_data/README.md.

